## Detailed Verification Log

### Pre‑liminaries
The problem asks for all non‑negative integers \(k\) such that, for a given \(n\ge 3\), there exist \(n\) distinct lines covering every lattice point \((a,b)\) with \(a,b>0\) and \(a+b\le n+1\), and exactly \(k\) of those lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \(x\)-axis, the \(y\)-axis, or the line \(x+y=0\)).  

The solution introduces the set  
\[
S_n=\{(a,b)\in\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid a+b\le n+1\},
\qquad |S_n|=\frac{n(n+1)}2 .
\]

A line is sunny iff its slope is different from \(0,\infty,-1\).

---

### Section 1 – Reduction to the triangle \(T_k\)

**Claim.**  
Given any configuration with exactly \(k\) sunny lines, we may replace each non‑sunny line by a vertical line \(x=c\) (with distinct \(c\)’s) without losing coverage, and we may assume the non‑sunny lines are precisely  
\[
x=1,\;x=2,\dots ,x=n-k .
\tag{1.1}
\]

**Analysis.**  
The argument is: a vertical line covers an entire column, while a horizontal line or a line of slope \(-1\) meets any given column in at most one point; therefore we can “move” each non‑sunny line to a fresh column.  

*Missing justification.*  
It is not shown that after moving a non‑sunny line to a new column all points previously covered by that line remain covered by the other lines (sunny or remaining non‑sunny). The original horizontal or \(-1\)‑slope line may have covered points in many columns; a single vertical line can cover only the points in one column. The solution assumes that the other lines will automatically cover the lost points, but no argument is provided. Consequently the reduction is not rigorously established.

**Classification.** Justification Gap.

---

### Section 2 – A bound for a sunny line inside \(T_k\)

The triangle after the reduction is  
\[
T_k=\{(x,y)\in\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid x+y\le k+1\}.
\tag{1.2}
\]

**Derivation.**  
A sunny line is written as  
\[
p x-q y=c,\qquad \gcd(p,q)=1,\;q>0,\;p\neq0,\;p\neq -q .
\tag{2.1}
\]
All integer points on it are  
\[
(x,y)=(x_0+qt,\;y_0+pt),\qquad t\in\mathbb Z .
\tag{2.2}
\]
Hence  
\[
x+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .
\tag{2.3}
\]

Because the line is sunny, \(p+q\neq0\). The sum therefore changes by \(|p+q|\) when \(t\) changes by \(1\).

Inside \(T_k\) we have \(2\le x+y\le k+1\). The solution concludes that the number of admissible integers \(t\) is at most  

\[
\Bigl\lfloor\frac{k+1-2}{|p+q|}\Bigr\rfloor+1
\le
\Bigl\lfloor\frac{k-1}{2}\Bigr\rfloor+1
=
\Bigl\lfloor\frac{k+1}{2}\Bigr\rfloor .
\tag{2.4}
\]

**Problematic part – the case \(|p+q|=1\).**  
When \(|p+q|=1\) the solution argues:

* “\(p+q=1\) forces \(q\ge2\) (otherwise \(p=0\) which would give a horizontal line).  
* Because \(x=x_0+qt\) must stay in \([1,k]\), the number of admissible \(t\) is at most \(\bigl\lfloor k/q\bigr\rfloor+1\le\bigl\lfloor(k+1)/2\bigr\rfloor\).”

The inequality \(\bigl\lfloor k/q\bigr\rfloor+1\le\bigl\lfloor(k+1)/2\bigr\rfloor\) is **not proved** and is in fact false for some admissible values (e.g. \(k=4,\;q=2\) gives \( \lfloor 4/2\rfloor+1 =3\) while \(\lfloor(4+1)/2\rfloor =2\)). The solution does not address the additional restriction that \(y\) must also stay positive and that the sum \(x+y\) must stay \(\le k+1\); these extra constraints are needed to obtain the claimed bound, but they are omitted.

Thus the bound \(|\text{sunny line}\cap T_k|\le\bigl\lfloor\frac{k+1}{2}\bigr\rfloor\) (Equation (2.5)) is not rigorously established for the \(|p+q|=1\) case.

**Classification.** Justification Gap.

